<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.type.watch" />

  <!--
    READ THIS BEFORE WIRING UP THE PROMPT.

    This permission is one-shot and unrecoverable. Once the user denies it, it
    CANNOT be re-requested, and setWatchFaceAsActive() may only ever be called
    once regardless. Deny it and that user is permanently stuck setting faces
    by hand via long-press.

    It is the only irreversible action in the whole system. Design where in the
    flow you ask before you write the code that asks. See docs/SPEC.md.
  -->
  <!--
    Required to call the Watch Face Push API AT ALL. Distinct from the
    activation permission below, and easy to miss: the library's own manifest
    declares no permission, only a <queries> entry, so nothing in the build
    tells you this is needed.

    Without it, every Push call fails with

      SecurityException: Not allowed to bind to service Intent {
        act=com.google.wear.ACTION_PUSH_WATCH_FACES ... }

    which names bindService rather than a missing permission. Read off the
    watch itself on 2026-08-29 — `dumpsys package
    com.google.android.wearable.dwf.receiver` shows WatchFaceReceiverService
    guarded by exactly this string. See DECISIONS.md.
  -->
  <!--
    Needed to post the notification that carries the activation ask. Android
    will not let the install path open the dialog directly — the face lands in a
    background context and the launch is blocked — so the notification IS the
    route to the one irreversible question. Without this permission `notify` is
    a silent no-op and the ask simply never happens.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

  <uses-permission android:name="com.google.wear.permission.PUSH_WATCH_FACES" />

  <uses-permission android:name="com.google.wear.permission.SET_PUSHED_WATCH_FACE_AS_ACTIVE" />

  <application
      android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
      android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
      android:label="BFG Watch Faces"
      android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
      android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round">
    <!--
      TRUE, and this is what stops the app appearing in the Play Store on a
      watch when it is false.

      "Non-standalone" is the Wear 2.x arrangement: the watch app was not
      independently installable and Play pushed it out automatically when the
      phone app was installed. Wear OS 3 removed that delivery, so a
      non-standalone app is simply not offered on a modern watch — which is
      exactly the symptom, an app released to a track that never appears in the
      list.

      It was false because when it was written this app had no launcher activity
      at all: there was nothing to open and nothing to install on its own. That
      stopped being true when WatchActivity landed. The app now installs, opens,
      and tells you whether it is ready and whether it may switch your face. It
      needs the phone app to be USEFUL, which is a different question from
      whether it can stand on its own.
    -->
    <meta-data android:name="com.google.android.wearable.standalone" android:value="true" />

    <!--
      Receives a face from the device over ChannelClient and installs it.
      addWatchFace takes a ParcelFileDescriptor — a LOCAL file — and there is
      no "send to the paired watch" call anywhere in the API, so something on
      the watch has to be holding the bytes. That is the whole reason this app
      exists; see docs/SPEC.md, "Why there are two apps".
    -->
    <service
        android:name=".FaceReceiverService"
        android:exported="true">
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="com.google.android.gms.wearable.CHANNEL_EVENT" />
        <data android:scheme="wear" android:host="*" android:pathPrefix="/bfg-watchfaces" />
      </intent-filter>
    </service>

    <!--
      Hosts the one irreversible permission request, which Android only allows
      from an Activity. No layout: the system dialog is the interface, and the
      explanation already happened on the device before the face was sent.
    -->
    <!--
      No android:noHistory here, and it must not come back. It finishes this
      activity as soon as the permission dialog covers it, so the result
      callback never arrives and the one irreversible answer is silently lost.
      Observed on Wear OS 6, 2026-08-29 — see ActivationRequestActivity.
    -->
    <!--
      The launcher entry. Until 2026-08-29 there was none: the app was a
      listener service and a permission activity, so installed from Play it
      was invisible and a tester had no way to open it or tell whether it
      worked. See WatchActivity.
    -->
    <activity
        android:name=".WatchActivity"
        android:exported="true"
        android:label="BFG Watch Faces"
        android:taskAffinity="">
      <!--
        Opened BY THE PHONE, through RemoteActivityHelper, at the one moment it
        matters: a face has landed and the activation permission has not been
        asked for.

        Without this the wearer had to know, unprompted, to open an app on
        their watch. The permission can only be requested on the watch, the ask
        is a notification, and a fresh install holds no notification permission
        either — so the first face installed, nothing switched, and no prompt
        of any kind could appear. Measured on a Pixel Watch 5, 2026-09-01:
        "notifications are not enabled; the activation ask cannot be shown".

        RemoteActivityHelper requires ACTION_VIEW with BROWSABLE and a data
        URI, which is what this filter is.
      -->
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
        <data android:scheme="bfgwatchfaces" android:host="setup" />
      </intent-filter>
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
      </intent-filter>
    </activity>

    <activity
        android:name=".ActivationRequestActivity"
        android:exported="false"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar"
        android:excludeFromRecents="true" />
  </application>
</manifest>
